{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Added a per-feed `fetch_timeout` (integer seconds, 0 = use the global timeout). Build, unit tests and integration tests pass; the new behaviour itself is not exercised by the existing tests.\n\n- **Storage:** new migration adds `feeds.fetch_timeout int not null default 0`; the column is read in the feed query and written by `CreateFeed` and `UpdateFeed`.\n- **API:** `fetch_timeout` appears in the feed object and is accepted by `PUT /v1/feeds/{feedID}`. The Go client's `Feed` and `FeedModificationRequest` carry it too.\n- **Fetching:** new `Feed.HTTPClientTimeout(global)` returns the per-feed value when it is above 0, otherwise the global one. It is used for the feed refresh, the entry processor / web-page scraper, and the icon fetch.\n\nThings you might not expect:\n- Negative values are rejected by `ValidateFeedModification` with a plain English message rather than a translation key, to avoid touching the 26 locale files.\n- The field is not settable at feed creation (`POST /v1/feeds`) or in the web UI edit form; the UI form leaves an existing value untouched.\n- Subscription discovery and the first fetch at feed creation still use the global timeout, since no feed row exists yet.", "t": 1794462139.803}
{"tool": "_result", "name": "finish", "chars": 55, "t": 1794462139.803}
